---
import Guide from '@/layouts/Guide.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'
import Callout from '@/components/guides/Callout.astro'

const tagTs = `await mcpd.withSpan('answer_question', async (run) => {
  run.setCorrelation({ userId: customer.id })
  // ... your model calls, recorded as model_generation spans
})`

const tagPy = `with mcpd.trace("answer_question", user_id=customer.id) as run:
    ...  # your model calls, recorded as model_generation spans`

const tagOtel = `# On the root span of the run, with any OpenTelemetry SDK.
span.set_attribute("user.id", customer.id)`

const daemon = `# The key mcpd hashes user ids with. Keep it as secret as a password.
mcpd --redact-key "$MCPD_REDACT_KEY"`

const cost = `curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/analytics/cost \\
  --data-urlencode 'project=support' \\
  --data-urlencode 'from=-30d' \\
  --data-urlencode 'group_by=user_id' \\
  | jq -c '.groups[] | {user: .key, runs: .count, cost: .total_cost, input_tokens, output_tokens}'`

const costTs = `const res = await fetch(
  'http://localhost:4317/v1/api/analytics/cost?' +
    new URLSearchParams({ project: 'support', from: '-30d', group_by: 'user_id' }),
)
const { groups, currency } = await res.json()
for (const g of groups) console.log(g.key, g.count, g.total_cost, currency)`

const costPy = `import requests

res = requests.get(
    "http://localhost:4317/v1/api/analytics/cost",
    params={"project": "support", "from": "-30d", "group_by": "user_id"},
)
body = res.json()
for g in body["groups"]:
    print(g["key"], g["count"], g["total_cost"], body["currency"])`

const digestPy = `import hashlib


def mcpd_digest(user_id: str, redact_key: str) -> str:
    """The value mcpd stores for user_id under that redact key."""
    raw = hashlib.sha256(redact_key.encode() + user_id.encode()).hexdigest()
    return "sha256:" + raw[:24]


# Map the report back to your own customers.
names = {mcpd_digest(c.id, REDACT_KEY): c.email for c in customers}`

const digestTs = `import { createHash } from 'node:crypto'

/** The value mcpd stores for userId under that redact key. */
function mcpdDigest(userId: string, redactKey: string): string {
  const raw = createHash('sha256').update(redactKey).update(userId).digest('hex')
  return 'sha256:' + raw.slice(0, 24)
}

// Map the report back to your own customers.
const names = new Map(customers.map((c) => [mcpdDigest(c.id, REDACT_KEY), c.email]))`

const digestSh = `printf '%s%s' "$MCPD_REDACT_KEY" "customer-7" | sha256sum | cut -c1-24 | sed 's/^/sha256:/'`

const oneUser = `# One customer's most expensive runs this month. Pass the plain id.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/traces \\
  --data-urlencode 'project=support' \\
  --data-urlencode 'user_id=customer-7' \\
  --data-urlencode 'from=-30d' \\
  --data-urlencode 'sort=cost' \\
  | jq -r '.traces[] | [.trace_id, .name, .cost.total_cost] | @tsv'`
---

<Guide
  slug="llm-cost-per-user"
  lede="mcpd prices every model call when it arrives. Put the end user's id on each run, and one API call gives you cost and tokens per user over any window."
  related={['log-openai-api-calls', 'trace-chat-conversations', 'slack-alerts']}
>
  <p>
    When you finish, you can see what each of your customers cost you in model
    calls this month, and open the runs behind any figure. You need runs in mcpd
    whose model calls carry token counts: the
    <a href="/docs/guides/log-openai-api-calls" class="link">OpenAI guide</a> and
    the framework guides set them up.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Put the user's id on every run</h2>
  <p>
    Set the user id on the root span of each run. With an SDK it is a
    correlation field. With OpenTelemetry, mcpd reads <code>user.id</code>,
    <code>enduser.id</code> or <code>mcpd.user_id</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="tag the run"
    samples={[
      { lang: 'typescript', label: 'TypeScript', shiki: 'typescript', code: tagTs },
      { lang: 'python', label: 'Python', shiki: 'python', code: tagPy },
    ]}
  />
  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="with OpenTelemetry"
    samples={[{ lang: 'python', label: 'Python', shiki: 'python', code: tagOtel }]}
  />
  <p>
    Use an id that does not change, such as your customer id. Do not use an
    email address: run names and log messages are not redacted, and a stable id
    is all the report needs.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Set the key mcpd hashes user ids with</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd does not store the id you send. At ingest it replaces it with a keyed
    digest, <code>sha256:</code> and 24 hex characters. The same id always gives
    the same digest under one key, so grouping and filtering still work, and the
    stored data does not say who the person is.
  </p>
  <p>
    The built-in key is in the public source, so set your own in any real
    deployment:
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" title="start the daemon" samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'Shell', shiki: 'bash', code: daemon }]} />
  <Callout tone="warning">
    Changing the key later changes every digest. Runs stored before the change no
    longer group with runs stored after it. See
    <a href="/docs/privacy">Redaction and retention</a>.
  </Callout>

  <h2>3. Get cost per user</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/analytics/cost</code> with <code>group_by=user_id</code>
    returns one row per user: the number of runs, the cost, and the input and
    output tokens. Runs sent with no user id share the row
    <code>(none)</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="cost per user, last 30 days"
    samples={[
      { lang: 'curl', label: 'cURL', shiki: 'bash', code: cost },
      { lang: 'typescript', label: 'TypeScript', shiki: 'typescript', code: costTs },
      { lang: 'python', label: 'Python', shiki: 'python', code: costPy },
    ]}
  />
  <TerminalCast
    class="not-prose my-8"
    title="cost per user"
    lines={[
      { cmd: "curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/analytics/cost --data-urlencode 'project=support' --data-urlencode 'from=-30d' --data-urlencode 'group_by=user_id' | jq -c '.groups[] | {user: .key, runs: .count, cost: .total_cost, input_tokens, output_tokens}'" },
      { out: '{"user":"sha256:9c23932e466b80d09d174f67","runs":5,"cost":0.00038399999999999996,"input_tokens":1624,"output_tokens":74}', tone: 'ok' },
      { out: '{"user":"(none)","runs":2,"cost":0.00038399999999999996,"input_tokens":1624,"output_tokens":74}' },
      { out: '{"user":"sha256:dcce23461766dc17e1d4c11d","runs":1,"cost":0.0,"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}', tone: 'ok' },
    ]}
  />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Values</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>from</code>, <code>to</code></td><td>RFC 3339 or relative, such as <code>-30d</code>. The default window is the last seven days.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>group_by</code></td><td><code>user_id</code> here. Also <code>model</code>, <code>provider</code>, <code>session_id</code>, <code>environment</code> and more.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>bucket</code></td><td>Add <code>1d</code> for a daily series per user next to the totals.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The same breakdown is in the interface: open <strong>Spend</strong> and group
    by <strong>user</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Turn digests back into your users</h2>
  <p>
    The report shows digests. Your application knows the ids and the key, so it
    can compute the same digest for each customer and look the rows up. The
    digest is the first 12 bytes of SHA-256 over the redact key followed by the
    user id:
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="the digest mcpd stores"
    samples={[
      { lang: 'python', label: 'Python', shiki: 'python', code: digestPy },
      { lang: 'typescript', label: 'TypeScript', shiki: 'typescript', code: digestTs },
      { lang: 'curl', label: 'Shell', shiki: 'bash', code: digestSh },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    For one customer you do not need the digest at all. The trace list hashes a
    <code>user_id</code> filter the same way before it matches, so you pass the
    plain id:
  </p>
  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" title="one customer's runs" samples={[{ lang: 'curl', label: 'cURL', shiki: 'bash', code: oneUser }]} />

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What you see</th><th>Why, and what to do</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Your digests do not match the report</td>
        <td>
          The daemon runs with a different <code>--redact-key</code> than the one you hash with, or the
          key changed since those runs were stored. A daemon started with <code>--no-redact</code> stores
          plain ids.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A user's cost is lower than the provider's bill</td>
        <td>
          Some calls have no cost: their model is not in mcpd's price book. mcpd leaves an unknown model
          unpriced rather than at zero. Check with <code>mcpd prices show &lt;model&gt;</code> and see
          <a href="/docs/cost" class="link">Cost accounting</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>A large share of the cost is in the row <code>(none)</code></td>
        <td>Those runs were sent without a user id. Set it on the root span of every run.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Guide>
